{"ts":"2026-07-30T17:21:52.248Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_instance":"6682b76673432e27b62e2ad8","agent_provenance":{"model":null,"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"}},"op":"learning_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-07-30T17:21:52.248Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/learnings","value":[{"created_at":"2026-07-30T17:21:52.247Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Measurement discipline this issue depends on, worth stating because the intuitive measurement hides the defect: judge a bounded read by the cost of the whole answer, never by the cost of one page. Per page the triage projection looks like a clear win at 6,250 bytes against 71,709 for the unprojected call. Multiplied by the pages needed to reach the same 315 rows it is 987,500 bytes, and the win becomes a 13.8-fold loss. The comparison that matters is total tokens to answer the question the caller actually asked.\n\nThe derived quantity to record for any paginated surface is bytes per row at the offered page size, because it exposes envelope amortisation directly: 228 bytes per row unprojected against 3,125 per row at a two-row page is the whole finding in one ratio, and it stays meaningful across corpus sizes in a way that a page byte count does not."}]}],"before_hash":"3b160bddebc1ae46f7ae2fac10d3428ed480e3b29fb03c506f0129d9afa5ec98","after_hash":"88a42a2fd3c5b783236a840f9ef4887ec248efea357eaa475eecbb0aa981a987"}
